#include <string>

std::unique_ptr<Position> open_position(std::string sym, int qty) {
    if (qty == 0) return nullptr;
    return std::make_unique<Position>(sym, qty);
}

screens.prose:
`std::shared_ptr` lets several owners share one object. It keeps a **count** of owners and deletes the object when the count reaches 0. Every copy changes the count with an atomic instruction, which is why desks keep it out of hot paths.

```cpp
auto a = std::make_shared<Order>();
{
    auto b = a;
    auto c = b;
    // here
}
```

Two nodes that own each other, as in a book linked to its strategy and back.

```cpp
struct Node {
    std::shared_ptr<Node> other;
    ~Node() { std::cout << "freed\n"; }
};
int main() {
    {
        auto a = std::make_shared<Node>();
        auto b = std::make_shared<Node>();
        a->other = b;
        b->other = a;
    }
    std::cout << "end of main\n";
}
```

Build classes from members that own themselves, and write no cleanup at all. This is the **rule of zero**.

```cpp
struct Desk {
    std::vector<Order> orders;       // owns its elements
    std::unique_ptr<Pricer> pricer;  // owns one Pricer
    std::string name;                // owns its text
    // no destructor, no delete: each member frees itself
};
```

`Position` prints `open` in its constructor and `close` in its destructor.

```cpp
void hold(std::unique_ptr<Position> p) {
    std::cout << "hold " << p->qty << "\n";
}
int main() {
    auto p = std::make_unique<Position>("VOD", 100);
    std::cout << "opened\n";
    hold(std::move(p));
    std::cout << (p == nullptr) << " after hold\n";
}
```

